ds were actually very very  effective  however what happened just recently is  all of a sudden  after having such success with methods  that we already discussed covid19 has  surpassed the mortality of even such  diseases like cancer  and so on one hand we have this dynamic  where we have effective measures that  are quite simple  just washing your hands having chlorine  and water having availability of  antibiotics and then all of a sudden we  see this tremendous  growth of mortality caused by a virus a  virus that has already been studied and  so that leads us to the significance and  the impact of bioinformatics  so in the era of covet 19  not only were we able to analyze that  data quite uh rapidly but very quickly  we were able to identify a specific  pathogen that then was tracked almost in  real time to understand how it changes  how it evolves how it starts infecting  more and more people and starting to ask  important questions and the ability to  ask those questions using data that was  collected is the essence of  bioinformatics what is bioinformatics  bioinformatics allows us to leverage  these data sets that have been collected  ask specific questions and get to a  practical interpretation so that we  understand something and we're able to  make conclusions  now importantly this pandemic has opened  a  number of new questions that are now  being asked and we will see how those  questions are being asked by researchers  that have  the expertise to design experiments or  to leverage data that was collected from  others  so here's an example an example where we  can see that we can not only detect a  specific strain of a virus but we can  actually track it over time and  important questions will start to emerge  what is the long-term consequences of  covet-19 how do we monitor and prevent  outbreaks of these infections how do we  leverage our detailed genomic  characterization of the virus or the  pathogen and introduce a very specific  targeted therapy th